<Article title="How CAN Bus Load Is Estimated" description="Understand Classical CAN overhead, bit stuffing, identifier formats, and the limits of bus-load estimates."><h2>Capacity is consumed by complete frames</h2><p>CAN traffic includes arbitration, control, CRC, acknowledgement, delimiters, payload, and intermission. This toolkit models 47 base bits for an 11-bit identifier frame or 67 for a 29-bit identifier frame, then adds eight bits per payload byte.</p><div class="formula">load % = frame bits × frames/second ÷ bit rate × 100</div><h2>Bit stuffing makes length data-dependent</h2><p>CAN inserts a complementary bit after five consecutive equal-polarity bits through the stuffed region. The actual count depends on the transmitted pattern. The calculator therefore reports an unstuffed baseline and a simple planning estimate adding 20% and rounding up; neither should be presented as an exact trace.</p><h2>Worked example: a 500 kbit/s Classical CAN stream</h2><h3>Scenario and inputs</h3><p>An 11-bit standard-identifier Classical CAN data frame carries 8 data bytes and is sent 1,000 times per second on a 500 kbit/s bus. This is a theoretical capacity estimate, not a bus-analyzer measurement. The frame-length convention below includes the minimum 3-bit intermission.</p><h3>Formula and substitution</h3><ol><li>Unstuffed frame estimate = 47 base bits + (8 bytes × 8 bits/byte) = <strong>111 bits</strong>. The 47-bit base includes SOF, arbitration/control, CRC/ACK/EOF fields and the 3-bit intermission used by this calculator.</li><li>Unstuffed frame time = 111 bits ÷ 500,000 bit/s = <strong>222 μs</strong>.</li><li>Unstuffed load = 111 × 1,000 ÷ 500,000 × 100 = <strong>22.2%</strong>.</li><li>Planning allowance = ceil(111 × 1.20) = <strong>134 bits</strong>; conservative time = 134 ÷ 500,000 = <strong>268 μs</strong>; conservative load = 134 × 1,000 ÷ 500,000 × 100 = <strong>26.8%</strong>.</li></ol><p>The 20% figure is this site's deliberately simple planning allowance, not a protocol-defined strict worst-case bound. Actual dynamic stuffing depends on the transmitted bit pattern and applies only to the stuffed portion; retransmissions, error frames, arbitration delay, overload handling, and other traffic are not included. This example is Classical CAN only and does not use CAN FD fields or formulas.</p><h3>Conditions, limits, and references</h3><p>Use the baseline only for an early estimate with the stated standard frame and minimum intermission convention. For a detailed schedule, include every message stream and perform priority/response-time analysis. Bosch's <a href="https://tekeye.uk/downloads/can2spec.pdf" rel="noopener">CAN Specification 2.0</a> describes the frame fields, interframe spacing, and bit-stuffing rule; CiA's <a href="https://www.can-cia.org/can-knowledge/can-cc" rel="noopener">CAN CC overview</a> confirms the 8-byte Classical CAN data field and 3-bit intermission.</p><h2>Average load is not latency</h2><p>High-priority frames win arbitration, and bursts or error recovery can delay low-priority traffic even when average load is below 100%. Select an operating target from message deadlines and worst-case response analysis, not a universal rule of thumb.</p><h2>Validate the design</h2><p>Sum every periodic and event-driven stream, include diagnostics and boot traffic, then measure a stressed network with a CAN analyzer. Use a message-level timing analysis for safety-relevant systems.</p><p><a class="button" href="/tools/can-bus-load/">Open the CAN calculator</a></p></Article>
